Attacked D-2026-08-29a / issue #24 (require expected_scenario on POST /whatif/apply). All attack surfaces refuted; no defect found within scope.

1. Bypass hunt. `grep -rn "ApplyOverrides(" --include=*.go` finds exactly two
   non-test call sites: handleWhatIfApply (guarded, this diff) and
   internal/services/mcpsvc/plan/register.go:236 (apply_changes MCP tool,
   calls SettingsManager.ApplyOverrides directly — never goes through the
   HTTP handler at all). No JS/template caller of POST /whatif/apply exists
   (grepped web/static/js and web/templates — none). This matches
   X-RUN-SPEC.md's own framing ("Read and cite the one live caller
   (apply_changes in mcpsvc)") and settings.go:719's comment calling it
   "the MCP /whatif/apply path" — the spec already treats apply_changes as
   the relevant caller despite the different code path, so this is not a
   worker error, just confirmed as pre-existing framing.

2. Guard robustness — wrong JSON type. Live-probed (temp test, removed
   after) `expected_scenario: 5`, `null`, `[]`: all three return 400, no
   panic. Numbers/arrays fail json.Decode's UnmarshalTypeError before the
   guard runs (400 "invalid overrides JSON"); `null` decodes as a no-op onto
   the string zero value, so it falls through to the TrimSpace=="" guard
   (400 naming expected_scenario). Both paths are safe and 400.

3. Whitespace matching active scenario. handleWhatIfApply only TrimSpaces
   for the *emptiness* check; the raw (untrimmed) value is passed to
   ApplyOverrides. settings.go:1164 compares
   `expectedScenario != "" && expectedScenario != sm.filename` — exact
   string equality, no trim. So `" whatif.json"` (leading space) against
   active "whatif.json" is a mismatch → 409, nothing written. Confirmed
   deliberate and safe, matches the attack prompt's predicted (fine) outcome.

4. apply_changes / ActiveFilename() empty-string risk. Read
   NewSettingsManager (settings.go:128): `filename: defaultWhatIfFilename`
   ("whatif.json") — never empty at construction. Every reassignment site
   (SwitchScenario, scenario-delete revert, scenario-load reconciliation)
   sets it only after TrimSpace + existence validation, never to "". So
   ActiveFilename() cannot return "", and apply_changes's
   `expected := in.Scenario; if expected == "" { expected =
   deps.Settings.ActiveFilename() }` fallback can never send "" to
   ApplyOverrides. Claim confirmed, not just cited.

5. Coverage. `go test ./internal/services/mcpsvc/plan/...` passes but no
   test in that package exercises the in.Scenario=="" fallback branch
   itself (only confirmed by reading, per point 4, that it's unreachable
   with "" — a coverage gap, not a correctness defect; noting for the
   backlog, not grounds for FAIL since the fallback is provably dead for
   the reason above).

6. Pre-existing test dilution. `git diff master -- handlers_live_test.go`:
   all five patched tests (Persists/PositiveRoth/ZeroRoth/SpouseClaimAge/
   RejectsUnwritableAndInvalid) purely ADD `"expected_scenario": <active>`
   to existing request bodies — no assertion was removed or weakened. The
   409-mismatch and matching-scenario tests (MismatchedExpectedScenarioIs409,
   MatchingExpectedScenarioIsAccepted) predate this diff and are untouched.
   handlers_test.go and spending_trajectory_test.go also show diffs in the
   working tree, but git-diffing them confirms they belong to X6 (#25
   HX-Trigger) and X8 (green-700 sweep) respectively — out of X7's manifest
   scope (.swarm/manifests/X7.1.files lists only the 3 X7 files).

7. No-write claim — mutation-tested. Patched a copy of handlers_live.go so
   the guard's http.Error still fires but ApplyOverrides runs unconditionally
   beforehand (write-before-guard bypass). Ran
   TestHandleWhatIfApply_{Missing,Blank}ExpectedScenarioIs400AndWritesNothing
   against the mutant: both FAILED ("a rejected apply still wrote:
   monthly_living_expenses 4000 -> 9999"), confirming the tests' disk-state
   assertion actually catches a write-before-guard defect. Reverted to the
   original diff before continuing.

8. Baseline (binding). In the cp -a copy: `go build ./...` clean;
   `go test ./...` full suite green (54 packages, incl.
   internal/handlers/whatif, internal/services/mcpsvc/plan,
   internal/services/retirement — all ok). `go test -race -count=1
   ./internal/handlers/whatif/...` green (39s). `go vet` clean on the three
   touched packages; `gofmt -l` clean on the three X7 files. Note: per
   ruling X-2026-08-29a this run's ledger only requires package-scope
   verification given the shared tree with the concurrent W run — full
   ./... build+test above is strictly stronger and also passed, so the
   stricter bar is met regardless.

No FAIL-worthy defect found inside X7's scope. The one open item (#5,
